Recommending items to your users involves these steps:
  1. [Prepare your data](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/algolia-recommend/how-to/set-up#prepare-your-data)
  2. [Select and train the Recommend models](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/algolia-recommend/how-to/set-up#train-the-recommend-models)
  3. [Show recommendations in your user interface](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/algolia-recommend/how-to/set-up#integrate-recommend-into-your-user-interface)


##  Prepare your data
Your data is the foundation of the recommendations. Your **Algolia index** is the source of the recommendations, and **events** indicate which recommendations are most relevant. To learn more, see:
  * [Index your data](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/sending-and-managing-data/send-and-update-your-data)
  * [Send click and conversion events](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/sending-events/getting-started)
  * [Algolia Recommend for Magento](https://www.algolia.com/doc/integration/magento-2/how-it-works/recommend)
  * [Algolia Recommend for BigCommerce](https://www.algolia.com/doc/integration/bigcommerce/search-settings/recommend)
  * [Algolia Recommend for Salesforce B2C Commerce](https://www.algolia.com/doc/integration/salesforce-commerce-cloud-b2c/guides/algolia-recommend)


###  Collect click and conversion events for Algolia Recommend
To collect enough events for Algolia Recommend, check that you send these different events to Algolia:
  * Events related to Algolia—for example, on search results, and category pages
  * Events unrelated to Algolia—for example, product-detail pages, checkout pages

To speed up the collection of events, you can [upload past events with a CSV file](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/algolia-recommend/how-to/csv-upload). If you collect enough events but still get an error that prevents you from starting the training:
  * Check the [Events debugger](https://dashboard.algolia.com/events/debugger) in the Algolia dashboard for error messages.
  * Check that you set up the [`userToken`](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/sending-events/concepts/usertoken) correctly. Make sure you’re not using the same `userToken` for all users. For authenticated users, use the user ID as their `userToken` for Algolia. For non-authenticated users, set the `userToken` to a session-based ID. You can generate a random ID for each session and discard it once the session ends.


##  Train the Recommend models
If you have enough events, you can start training the Recommend models.
  1. Go to the [Algolia dashboard](https://dashboard.algolia.com/explorer/browse) and select your Algolia .
  2. On the left sidebar, select **Recommend**.
  3. Select the type of recommendations you want to generate and click **Start using**.
  4. In the **Select data source** section, select an index you want to use as a source for recommendations. By default, events associated with your data source index are used to train the model.
  5. Optional: add more sources for user events. Select additional indices or replicas from your app, or [upload a CSV file](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/algolia-recommend/how-to/csv-upload).
  6. Optional for Related Items: define key attributes for **content-based filtering**. Content-based filtering works best with attributes that contain textual information, such as the title or description. For more information, see [content-based filtering](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/algolia-recommend/overview#content-based-filtering-for-related-items-related-content).
  7. Optional for Trends: select which type of trend you want to use for recommendations.
     * **Trending items.** Generate recommendations from popular items in your product catalog. After selecting **Trending items** , you can select **Also enable trending items per facet values** to get trending items per facet value.
     * **Trending facet values.** Generate recommendations for popular facet values.
  8. Click **Start training**. Training a model can take up to two hours.
  9. When the training finishes, you can see a summary:

You can use more than one model to generate recommendations, and select the best recommendations for your use case. Models are [re-trained every day](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/algolia-recommend/overview#training-frequency).
##  Preview the recommendations
Once the model is trained, you can preview its recommendations by searching for items in your index. Each recommendation displays a confidence score from the model, ranging from 0 to 100. The closer the score is to 100, the more relevant the recommendations are.
##  Integrate Recommend into your user interface
To show recommendations in your app, you have the following options:
  * **React.** Use [React InstantSearch](https://www.algolia.com/doc/api-reference/widgets/react).
  * **Other JavaScript frontends.** Use [InstantSearch.js](https://www.algolia.com/doc/api-reference/widgets/js).
  * **Native app or backend implementation.** Use the [Recommend API client](https://www.algolia.com/doc/libraries/sdk/methods/recommend) or the [Recommend API](https://www.algolia.com/doc/rest-api/recommend).


###  Show recommendations in your user interface
To show recommendations in your user interface:
  * With InstantSearch.js
  * With React InstantSearch


  1. [Install and set up InstantSearch.js](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/building-search-ui/installation/js).
  2. Add a container element for your recommendations to your HTML.
  3. Add the widget for the Recommend model. You can also declare how each recommendation renders by providing an `item` template.

The following example adds recommendations from the Related Items model to an HTML element with the ID `relatedProducts`:
JavaScript

```
import algoliasearch from 'algoliasearch/lite';
import instantsearch from 'instantsearch.js';
import { relatedProducts } from 'instantsearch.js/es/widgets';

const searchClient = algoliasearch(
  'ALGOLIA_APPLICATION_ID',
  'ALGOLIA_SEARCH_API_KEY'
);

const search = instantsearch({
  indexName: 'instant_search',
  searchClient,
});

search.addWidgets(
  relatedProducts({
    container: '#relatedProducts',
    objectIDs: ['YOUR_PRODUCT_OBJECT_ID'],
    templates: {
      item(recommendation, { html }) {
        return html`
          <h2>${recommendation.name}</h2>
          <p>${recommendation.description}</p>

      },
    },
  });
);

search.start();

```

  1. [Install and set up React InstantSearch](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/building-search-ui/installation/react).
  2. Add the widget for the Recommend model. You can also declare how each recommendation renders by providing an `itemComponent` component.


React

```
import React from "react";
import algoliasearch from "algoliasearch/lite";
import { InstantSearch, RelatedProducts } from "react-instantsearch";

const searchClient = algoliasearch("ALGOLIA_APPLICATION_ID", "ALGOLIA_SEARCH_API_KEY");

function App() {
  return (
InstantSearch searchClient={searchClient} indexName="instant_search">
RelatedProducts
        objectIDs={["YOUR_PRODUCT_OBJECT_ID"]}
        itemComponent={Item}
      />
    </InstantSearch>
  );


function Item({ item }) {
  return (
    <>
h2>{item.name}</h2>
p>{item.description}</p>
    </>
  );


```

###  Refine your recommendations
Often, you want to refine your recommendations—for example, to only recommend in-stock products or to ensure all recommendations are from the same product category. To refine your recommendations, pass [`facetFilters`](https://www.algolia.com/doc/api-reference/api-parameters/facetFilters) to the component by using `queryParameters`.
JavaScript
React

```
relatedProducts({
  // ...
  queryParameters: {
    facetFilters: [`category:${selectedProduct.category}`, `inStock:true`],
  },
});

```


```
<RelatedProducts
  // ...
  queryParameters={{
    facetFilters: [`category:${selectedProduct.category}`, `inStock:true`],

/>;

```

To refine the recommendations by numeric values—for example, a price range, use [`numericFilters`](https://www.algolia.com/doc/api-reference/api-parameters/numericFilters). You can also apply [Rules](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/algolia-recommend/how-to/rules) to your recommendations for further refinement or manual curation.
###  Adjust the number of displayed recommendations
By default, Algolia Recommend shows up to:
  * 3 recommendations for Frequently Bought Together
  * 30 recommendations for other models

You can adjust this number by using the `limit` attribute.
JavaScript
React

```
relatedProducts({
  // ...
  limit: 5,
});

```


```
<RelatedProducts
  // ...
  limit={5}
/>;

```

###  Show fallback recommendations
Sometimes, Algolia Recommend can’t generate enough recommendations from the model. In that case, you can show fallback recommendations from your data source index instead. To show fallback recommendations, pass the [`facetFilters`](https://www.algolia.com/doc/api-reference/api-parameters/facetFilters) parameter to `fallbackParameters`. Fallback parameters are supported by the models: Frequently Bought Together, Related Items, Related Content, Trending Items and Looking Similar.
JavaScript
React

```
relatedProducts({
  // ...
  fallbackParameters: {
    facetFilters: [`category:${selectedProduct.category}`, `inStock:true`],
  },
});

```


```
<RelatedProducts
  // ...
  fallbackParameters={{
    facetFilters: [`category:${selectedProduct.category}`, `inStock:true`],

/>;

```

To pass numeric filters, use the [`numericFilters`](https://www.algolia.com/doc/api-reference/api-parameters/numericFilters) parameter. To increase the number of recommendations and coverage of your Related Items model, consider using [content-based filtering](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/algolia-recommend/overview#content-based-filtering-for-related-items-related-content) when training the model.
##  See also
For more information, see these blog articles:
  * [From search to recommendations](https://www.algolia.com/blog/product/from-search-to-recommendations-framing-your-customers-online-retail-journey)
  * [Six ways to use recommendations in ecommerce](https://www.algolia.com/blog/product/6-ways-to-leverage-recommendations-for-ecommerce-retailers)
  * [Why media content recommendations are important](https://www.algolia.com/blog/product/what-are-media-content-recommendations-and-why-are-they-important)


