Replace `INDEX_NAME` with the name of your index. Replace the `brand` attributes with one of your index’s [faceting attributes](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/managing-results/refine-results/faceting/how-to/declaring-attributes-for-faceting-with-dashboard). The **Filters** button reveals filters in a modal that slides from the bottom. It also uses the [`useCurrentRefinements`](https://www.algolia.com/doc/api-reference/widgets/current-refinements/react#hook) and [`useClearRefinements`](https://www.algolia.com/doc/api-reference/widgets/clear-refinements/react#hook) Hooks to display how many refinements are applied and let users clear them all at once.
##  Next steps
With this tutorial, you’ve got a solid starting point for building dynamic search interfaces. Some steps to improve the app are:
  * [Search in multiple indices](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/building-search-ui/ui-and-ux-patterns/multi-index-search/react) with [`Index`](https://www.algolia.com/doc/api-reference/widgets/index-widget/react).
  * Use a different component library like [Lottie React Native](https://github.com/lottie-react-native/lottie-react-native) or [React Native Elements](https://reactnativeelements.com/).
  * Discover more Hooks by checking the [API reference](https://www.algolia.com/doc/api-reference/widgets/react).


