This is the **React InstantSearch v7** documentation. If you’re upgrading from v6, see the [upgrade guide](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/building-search-ui/upgrade-guides/react#migrate-from-react-instantsearch-v6-to-react-instantsearch-v7). If you were using React InstantSearch Hooks, this v7 documentation applies—just check for [necessary changes](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/building-search-ui/upgrade-guides/react#migrate-from-react-instantsearch-hooks-to-react-instantsearch-v7). To continue using v6, you can find the [archived documentation](https://algolia.com/old-docs/deprecated/instantsearch/react/v6/api-reference/instantsearch/).
You may wish to query Algolia’s servers from your backend instead of the frontend, while still being able to use InstantSearch widgets. Possible motivations could be for security restrictions, for SEO purposes or to enrich the Algolia data with data from your servers. Keep in mind though that [Algolia recommends frontend search](https://support.algolia.com/hc/en-us/articles/4406981933457-Searching-from-the-frontend-or-the-backend-What-do-you-recommend-) for performance and high availability reasons.
##  How backend search works
InstantSearch is the UI on top of a search client. Its state is managed by the [Algolia search helper](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/building-search-ui/upgrade-guides/js#algolia-search-helper). However, you can use a different search client.
###  Algolia search client
Algolia’s [search client](https://github.com/algolia/algoliasearch-client-javascript) queries Algolia’s backend whenever users refine the search. The response from Algolia’s search client is a JSON object that includes:
  * Results already formatted for display (full record content, embedded HTML, image URLs, and so on).
  * Highlighting and snippeting
  * Pagination

To learn more, see [Backend search with an API client](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/building-search-ui/going-further/backend-search/in-depth/backend-search-with-an-api-client/react).
###  Custom search client
You can implement your own search client to query your backend which, in turn, uses the Algolia search client to query Algolia’s backend. To create your own client, you must implement an interface that receives and returns formatted data that InstantSearch can understand.
##  On the backend: create the necessary routes
This guide assumes that you have an existing server running on [`http://localhost:3000`](http://localhost:3000) with the route `POST /search` that takes the default Algolia parameters as JSON. This backend could be using the [JavasScript API client](https://www.algolia.com/doc/libraries/sdk/install) to query Algolia, on top of any other operations you want to perform. The `algoliasearch` package lets you query Algolia from your backend. Here’s an example using [Express](https://expressjs.com/):
JavaScript

```
// Instantiate an Algolia client
const algoliasearch = require("algoliasearch");
const algoliaClient = algoliasearch(
  "ALGOLIA_APPLICATION_ID",
  "ALGOLIA_SEARCH_API_KEY",
);

// Add the search endpoint
app.post("/search", async ({ body }, res) => {
  const { requests } = body;
  const results = await algoliaClient.search(requests);
  res.status(200).send(results);
});

// Add the recommendations endpoint
app.post("/getRecommendations", async ({ body }, res) => {
  const { requests } = body;
  const results = await algoliaClient.getRecommendations(requests);
});

```

Once your new route is ready, get back to the frontend and create a search client that can communicate with this server.
##  On the frontend: call your new backend routes
###  Searching for results
The `search()` method runs whenever users search or refine results. Because the server accepts requests in the InstantSearch format, forward the from this method to your backend and return the response.
JavaScript

```
const requestsCache = new Map();
const resultsCache = new Map();
function makeCacheKey(requests, target) {
  // You can go further here, like sorting individual values inside the requests.
  // This makse sure the key is always the same for the same requests.
  return target + JSON.stringify(requests);


const customSearchClient = {
  search(requests) {
    const key = makeCacheKey(requests, 'search');
    if (resultsCache.has(key)) {
      return Promise.resolve(resultsCache.get(key));

    let request;
    if (requestsCache.has(key)) {
      request = requestsCache.get(key)!;
else {
      request = fetch('http://localhost:3000/search', {
        method: 'post',
        headers: {
          'Content-Type': 'application/json',
        },
        body: JSON.stringify({ requests }),
      }).then(res => res.json());


    return request.then(results => {
      resultsCache.set(key, results);
      return results;
    });
  },
  getRecommendations(requests) {
   const key = makeCacheKey(requests, 'getRecommendations');
    if (resultsCache.has(key)) {
      return resultsCache.get(key);

    let request;
    if (requestsCache.has(key)) {
      request = requestsCache.get(key)!;
else {
      request = fetch('http://localhost:3000/getRecommendations', {
        method: 'post',
        headers: {
          'Content-Type': 'application/json',
        },
        body: JSON.stringify({ requests }),
      }).then(res => res.json());


    return request.then(results => {
      resultsCache.set(key, results);
      return results;
    });
  },
  clearCache() {
    requestsCache.clear();
    resultsCache.clear();
  },
};

```

This example uses the [Fetch API](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Fetch_API) to query the server. Check the [browser compatibility](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Fetch_API#Browser_compatibility) requirements before using it in production. If you want to transform the data passed to your server, you can learn more about the parameters that the `search` and `getRecommendations` methods support:
  * [Search API parameters](https://www.algolia.com/doc/api-reference/api-parameters)
  * [Recommendations API parameters](https://www.algolia.com/doc/rest-api/recommend/get-recommendations)


###  Search for facet values
The same `search()` method is used by InstantSearch to fetch values from refinement lists.
You must set the option [`searchable`](https://www.algolia.com/doc/api-reference/widgets/refinement-list/react#param-searchable) to `true` in your refinement list to make it searchable.
##  Use the search client with InstantSearch
Now, you need to tell InstantSearch to use the search client you created. This is possible with the `searchClient` option. This parameter turns off all Algolia requests coming from the frontend and proxies them to your backend and displays the UI.
React

```
import { InstantSearch } from "react-instantsearch";

const App = ({ children }) => (
InstantSearch searchClient={customSearchClient} indexName="YourIndexName">
    {children}
  </InstantSearch>
);

```

##  Enrich data from the backend
Now that InstantSearch is querying your backend before fetching results from Algolia, you could merge Algolia’s data with yours (to offer your users more exhaustive results). A recurring problem with ecommerce websites using Algolia managing the remaining stock for each product. It’s sometimes hard to keep track of the exact number of items. An approach made possible with a custom backend is to only store the item’s availability on each Algolia record (`none`, `low`, `medium`, `high`) and to fetch the exact stock on your database. You need to make a few changes to your backend:
JavaScript

```
app.post('/search', async ({body}, res) => {
  const requests = body;
  const algoliaResults = await algoliaClient.search(requests);
  const results = {
    ...algoliaResults,
    results: algoliaResults.results.map(result => ({
      ...result,
      hits: result.hits.map(async hit => ({
        ...hit,
        // `getStock()` retrieves a product's exact stock from your database
        stock: await getStock(hit.productID),
      })),
    })),
  };

  res.status(200).send(results);
});

app.post('/getRecommendations', async ({body, res) => {
  /* The same logic as above can be applied here */
});

```

You can now access the property `stock` on each hit with InstantSearch on the frontend.
