This is the **React InstantSearch v7** documentation. If you’re upgrading from v6, see the [upgrade guide](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/building-search-ui/upgrade-guides/react#migrate-from-react-instantsearch-v6-to-react-instantsearch-v7). If you were using React InstantSearch Hooks, this v7 documentation applies—just check for [necessary changes](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/building-search-ui/upgrade-guides/react#migrate-from-react-instantsearch-hooks-to-react-instantsearch-v7). To continue using v6, you can find the [archived documentation](https://algolia.com/old-docs/deprecated/instantsearch/react/v6/api-reference/instantsearch/).
Although Algolia provides analytics tailored to your search implementation, you might want to send your search data to your existing analytics tools. To do this, implement some custom middleware:
Set up Google Analytics
See [Set up the Google tag with gtag.js](https://developers.google.com/tag-platform/gtagjs).
Create the middleware to send events
In the event listener, send events to Google Analytics. This example debounces the event for 3 seconds but adjust this to your needs.
JavaScript

```
function googleAnalyticsMiddleware() {
  let timer;
  const sendEventDebounced = () => {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => {
      gtag('event', 'page_view', {
        page_location: window.location.pathname + window.location.search,
      });
    }, 3000);
  });

  return {
    onStateChange() {
      sendEventDebounced();
    },
    subscribe() {},
    unsubscribe() {},
  };


```

Inject the middleware into the InstantSearch lifecycle
React

```
import { useInstantSearch } from "react-instantsearch";

function Middleware() {
  const { addMiddlewares } = useInstantsearch();

  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    return addMiddlewares(googleAnalyticsMiddleware);
  }, []);

  return null;


function Search() {
  return (
InstantSearch searchClient={searchClient} indexName="instant_search">
Middleware />
    </InstantSearch>
  );


```

