Start the app
Start the development server.

```
npm run dev

```

Open [`http://localhost:5173`](http://localhost:5173) and start searching.
You see a product grid with images, a product type filter, and a search box. As you type, the product grid updates. Matching text is highlighted in the results.
You now have a working end-to-end search:
  * Records come from the `quickstart-products` index you created earlier.
  * Queries from `SearchBox` update the results and pagination together.
  * The `product_type` filter works because that attribute is configured for faceting.
  * `Highlight` and `Snippet` show which parts of each record match the query.


##  Improve relevance and add features
Improve relevance
Your app already returns matching products. You can improve relevance by adjusting which matches appear first.Update the existing `indexSettings` object in `scripts/indexing.ts`, then rerun `npm run index:products`.
scripts/indexing.ts

```
const { taskID } = await client.setSettings({
  indexName,
  indexSettings: {
    attributesForFaceting: ["product_type"],
    searchableAttributes: [ 
      "unordered(title)",
      "unordered(product_type)",
      "unordered(description)",
    ],
    customRanking: ["desc(units_sold)", "desc(price)"],
  },
});

```

See all 12 lines
This makes search results more useful by [prioritizing matches](https://www.algolia.com/doc/api-reference/api-parameters/searchableAttributes) in product titles and types before descriptions, then [ranking similar matches](https://www.algolia.com/doc/api-reference/api-parameters/customRanking) by best-selling and higher-priced products.
Add more search features
Add features that help users understand and refine results. For example, you can add [search stats](https://www.algolia.com/doc/api-reference/widgets/stats/react), [active filters](https://www.algolia.com/doc/api-reference/widgets/current-refinements/react), or more ways to filter results, such as [filter lists](https://www.algolia.com/doc/api-reference/widgets/refinement-list/react), [menus](https://www.algolia.com/doc/api-reference/widgets/menu/react), and [numerical filters](https://www.algolia.com/doc/api-reference/widgets/numeric-menu/react).
If you add more filtering features, update `attributesForFaceting` in `scripts/indexing.ts` to match.
Collect events from your search interface
Collect events from your UI to measure engagement and support [Algolia features that use behavioral data](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/sending-events/concepts/event-types).Update the `InstantSearch` component in `src/App.tsx`:
src/App.tsx

```
<InstantSearch
  indexName="quickstart-products"
  searchClient={searchClient}
  insights


```

This turns on the default React InstantSearch events, including `view` events for hits and filter click events for `RefinementList`.To send click events for hits, see [Send events with React InstantSearch](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/building-search-ui/events/react).
##  What’s next
  * [Get started with React InstantSearch](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/building-search-ui/getting-started/react) to continue building your search interface.
  * [Prepare your records for indexing](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/sending-and-managing-data/prepare-your-data) to replace the sample catalog with your own product data.
  * [Manage results](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/managing-results/relevance-overview) to learn how to adjust relevance and ranking to your needs.


