[Skip to main content](https://www.algolia.com/doc/libraries/sdk/v1/methods/copy-index#content-area)
**Required ACL:** `addObject` You can **copy the entire index** (records, settings, synonyms, and rules) or one or more of the following [scopes](https://www.algolia.com/doc/libraries/sdk/v1/methods/copy-index#scopes):
  * Settings
  * Synonyms
  * Rules

This method **doesn’t copy** the [`enableReRanking`](https://www.algolia.com/doc/api-reference/api-parameters/enableReRanking) and [`mode`](https://www.algolia.com/doc/api-reference/api-parameters/mode) settings.
###  Rate limiting
Copying an index is [rate-limited](https://support.algolia.com/hc/en-us/articles/4406975251089#when-do-we-trigger-the-rate-limit):
  * If you have more than 100 pending requests, your requests are throttled.
  * If you have more than 5,000 pending requests, further requests are ignored.


###  Source indices
Copying a source index that doesn’t exist creates a new index with 0 records. If the source index has replicas, the replicas won’t be copied.
###  Destination indices
When copying indices within the same Algolia application, the destination index is replaced if it exists. You can’t copy an index between two Algolia applications, if the destination index exists. Everything apart from the analytics data is replaced. You can’t copy to a destination index that already has replicas.
###  Analytics
Copying an index has no impact on Analytics. [You can’t copy an index’s analytics data](https://www.algolia.com/doc/guides/sending-and-managing-data/manage-indices-and-apps/manage-indices/concepts/indices-analytics).
###  Scopes
To copy parts of your source index, use the [`scope`](https://www.algolia.com/doc/libraries/sdk/v1/methods/copy-index#param-scope) parameter. If you omit the `scope` parameter, **everything is copied**. For example, to copy an index’s settings and synonyms, but not records and rules, set the `scope` parameter to: `["settings", "synonyms"]`.
  * The scope is replaced completely. Different items belonging to the same scope aren’t merged. For example, with `scope: "settings"`, all settings of the destination index are replaced with the settings of the source index.
  * Items in different scopes are preserved.
  * If you set the `scope` parameter, records aren’t copied.


